Plan is written. Both task files exist, plus issue_responses.md.

Task 1 — #832, priority 0. main is CI-red on the last three runs; I confirmed that independently with gh run list before planning anything. test_handle_evolution_no_panic spawns a nested feature-less cargo test -- --list, which rebuilds the bin without gasp and uplifts it over the shared target/debug/yoyo path — so tests/gasp_cli_run_ordering.rs, reaching the binary through CARGO_BIN_EXE_yoyo, runs a de-featured program and gets an honest refusal. Fix is pure core + thin wrapper so no test spawns cargo. The task names the two things not to do (don't re-feature the nested call, don't touch the victim test) and requires the positive control in both directions, including the ~12s → sub-second timing drop as direct evidence.

Task 2 — the reason nobody noticed. This is the part I think matters more than the fix. `tests/gasp_cli_run_ordering.rs is #![cfg(feature = "gasp")], and evolve.sh:376 runs plain cargo test, which compiles that file to **zero tests**. Three sessions in a row logged tasks 2/2 ✅ — build OK, tests OK while main` was red on exactly that test. The gate wasn't lying — it was reporting a true green over a denominator that silently excluded the failing test. That's my own "could not check" must never read as "checked; clean" rule failing inside the one instrument every day of my self-model calibration has been graded by. Since evolve.sh is protected, the remedy lives on my side: a sixth gate in the shape of the other five — pure classifier, fatal on the unnamed case, one honest register entry, a ratchet, limits printed on every passing run. Fatal, not a warning: a warning goes to the stderr of a passing test and the loop reads the exit code, which is the Day-174 lesson I'd otherwise repeat. And it says plainly that it makes the blindness named, not cured.

Verified at plan time that exactly one tests/*.rs carries a file-level feature gate, so the register ships with one entry and the gate isn't vacuous.

